// Yggdrasil stage-1 shake, as a Datalog rule set.
//
// This is the oracle half of docs/analysis-rules.md stage 1: it recomputes
// the shake's footprint from facts dumped by `yggdrasil facts PROG DIR`
// (yggdrasil.shen, (yggdrasil.facts ...)) and must agree, set for set, with
// the defuns the shake writes to kernel.kl -- excluding the synthesised
// shen.initialise, which is manufactured at write time and has no row in the
// call graph.
//
//     yggdrasil facts tests/fib.shen facts
//     souffle -F facts -D out analysis/analysis.dl
//     sort out/reach.csv
//
// Nothing here runs in a user's shake. Soufflé is a differential oracle in
// CI; analysis/refeval.py evaluates the same rules in stdlib Python for
// local runs. Both must produce the same reach set.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// DEVIATIONS FROM THE NOTE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// docs/analysis-rules.md was written from the design of the shake; these
// rules are written from `called-fns`, `prepare-tops`, `strip-f-error-row`
// and `trim-top` as they actually stand in yggdrasil.shen. Where the two
// disagree, the code wins and the divergence is recorded here.
//
// D1. The note's edge rules are position-sensitive:
//         edge(F,G) :- callpos(F,G), kernel(G).
//         edge(F,G) :- argpos(F,G,C), kernel(G), C != cons.
//     The code is not. `called-fns` is a plain cons walk that collects EVERY
//     kernel-defined symbol leaf of a body, in any position, cons arguments
//     included (docs/reachability.md calls this out as a deliberate
//     over-approximation). So the `C != cons` side condition is dropped:
//     argpos yields an edge unconditionally, exactly like callpos. The
//     callpos/argpos split is kept in the facts because it is what makes the
//     rules legible, and because stage 3 may want to narrow the edge rule --
//     but narrowing it today would shrink the footprint, which stage 1 is
//     not allowed to do.
//
// D2. The note gates data-literal symbols on eval-capability:
//         edge(F,G) :- datasym(F,G), kernel(G), evalcapable.
//     The code has no such gate. `called-fns`'s four exceptions -- the arity
//     table literal, the `put P shen.external-symbols` registration, a
//     toplevel `(declare F Type)`, and the `shen.*special*` /
//     `shen.*extraspecial*` lists, plus the `shen.assoc-> K` key -- drop
//     those symbols in BOTH modes. (What is eval-gated is what happens to
//     the surviving literals at write time: `trim-top` filters them to the
//     footprint, which cannot change the footprint.) So `datasym` derives no
//     edge at all. It is dumped and declared anyway: it is the exact record
//     of what the shake decided not to follow, which is the thing a reader
//     of the rule set most wants to see, and stage 3 will need it to prove
//     the exceptions are what the rules say they are.
//
// D3. The note has a fourth edge rule for shen.f-error:
//         edge(shen.f-error, G) :- callpos(shen.f-error, G), evalcapable.
//     That is right in spirit but incomplete: `strip-f-error-row` empties
//     shen.f-error's row wholesale in eval-free mode, so the argpos edges
//     have to go too. Both rules below carry the mode guard.
//
// D4. The note's `seed`/`floorseed` read `form-mentions(N, G)` from a single
//     set of toplevel forms. `prepare-tops` makes the toplevel forms
//     themselves mode-dependent: in eval-free mode it deletes every
//     `(declare F Type)` form and rewrites two more (`(set *macros* ...)` is
//     blanked, `(shen.build-lambda-table ...)` becomes a placeholder). The
//     dump therefore carries both readings, `formmentions` (raw) and
//     `formmentionsef` (post-prepare-tops), indexed by the same raw form
//     number, and the rules pick between them on the mode.
//
// D5. Likewise `usersym`. `strip-user-declares` drops user `(declare ...)`
//     forms in eval-free mode only, so the dump carries `usersym` (the
//     symbols of the user KL as the shake sees it in eval-free mode) and
//     `rawsym` (as it sees it in eval-capable mode). `evalcapable` is
//     derived from `rawsym`, because `eval-free?` is tested against the
//     UNstripped user KL.
//
// D6. `reach` is restricted to `kernel`. The Shen `footprint` puts every
//     seed in its visited set, kernel-defined or not; `footcode` then keeps
//     only the kernel defuns. Restricting the recursion is the same answer
//     one step earlier.
//
// D7. `initprim` is handed over as a fact rather than derived. `trim-top`
//     rewrites the arity table, the external-symbols list and the lambda
//     table against the FOOTPRINT, so the primitives the emitted initialiser
//     mentions depend on the answer this rule set computes. Deriving it
//     would mean restating `trim-arity-pairs`, `keep-set` and
//     `lambdatable-entries` in Datalog for no gain: `reach` is the stage-1
//     acceptance criterion, and `needsEval`/`reaches` ride along with it.
//     Note that `initprim` is consequently dumped for the mode the program
//     is actually in, unlike the other relations.
//
// D8. Stage 3 makes these rules the shake's own implementation, evaluated by
//     the `ygg.dl` engine in yggdrasil.shen over (value *shake-rules*) -- the
//     same rules, as Shen data, one clause per clause below. One thing the
//     rules do not and cannot give it: an ORDER. Datalog derives a set, and
//     `lambdatable-entries` walks the footprint LIST to build the
//     (set shen.*lambdatable* ...) literal, so an order has to come from
//     somewhere.
//
//     It is now DEFINED rather than walked. `ygg.rule-footprint` renders the
//     derived set as
//
//         [ F in kernel load order | reach(F) ]
//         ++ [ S in Seeds, deduplicated (last occurrence kept) |
//                                                      not reach(S) ]
//
//     where kernel load order is (map (fn row-head) Graph), i.e. the order
//     `graph-rows` read the kernel file in. The remainder is exactly the
//     non-kernel seeds -- primitives, user function names, data symbols --
//     which D6 excludes from `reach` and the Shen footprint has always
//     carried for `keep-set`/`trim-arity-pairs`/`eta-if-fn`.
//
//     Until this commit the shake instead re-ran the PRE-RULES depth-first
//     worklist over the raw call graph from its own seed list, emitted only
//     the nodes the rules admitted, and asserted with `ygg.dl-covered?` that
//     the two halves agreed. That was a second reachability implementation,
//     over a second graph, from a second seed computation, kept alive only
//     so that kernel.kl's bytes would not move; it is deleted, along with
//     `ygg.dl-walk-order`, `ygg.dl-succs`, `ygg.dl-edge?` and
//     `ygg.dl-covered?`. Membership is unchanged by construction: the walk
//     emitted F only when F was in reach u Seeds, emitted every seed, and
//     was asserted to emit every `reach` tuple, so its answer was reach u
//     Seeds as a set -- what the two halves above produce.
//
//     The order is observable in exactly one place: the
//     (set shen.*lambdatable* ...) literal inside the synthesised
//     shen.initialise. `footcode` selects kernel defuns by MEMBERSHIP and so
//     writes them in kernel load order whatever this list says, and `fn`
//     reads the lambda table with `assoc`, so the order carries no meaning
//     beyond those bytes. They moved once, at this commit: one line of
//     kernel.kl, a permutation of the same entries, on every eval-free
//     fixture (the eval-capable ones build the table at boot and are
//     byte-identical). Byte identity ACROSS PORTS is a guarantee; byte
//     identity across versions of Yggdrasil was a self-imposed constraint,
//     and it is given up here, deliberately, to stop paying for it with a
//     duplicate traversal. footprint_test.go's
//     TestFootprintOrderIsKernelLoadOrder checks the definition on every
//     fixture that shakes. Ordering is a value; membership is the rules'.

// ============================== facts ==================================

.decl kernel(f:symbol)                       // F is a kernel defun (a call-graph row head)
.input kernel

.decl callpos(f:symbol, g:symbol)            // G is the head cell of a cons inside F's body
.input callpos

.decl argpos(f:symbol, g:symbol, c:symbol)   // G is a later cell of a call whose head is C
.input argpos

.decl datasym(f:symbol, g:symbol)            // G sits in a subtree called-fns discards (D2)
.input datasym

.decl mentionsprim(f:symbol, p:symbol)       // primitive P occurs in kernel defun F's body
.input mentionsprim

.decl top(n:number, name:symbol)             // the N-th raw kernel toplevel init form
.input top

.decl formmentions(n:number, g:symbol)       // called-fns of raw form N
.input formmentions

.decl formmentionsef(n:number, g:symbol)     // called-fns of form N after prepare-tops (D4)
.input formmentionsef

.decl rawsym(s:symbol)                       // every symbol of the unstripped user KL
.input rawsym

.decl usersym(s:symbol)                      // every symbol of the declare-stripped user KL (D5)
.input usersym

.decl entry(s:symbol)                        // S is in *eval-entry-points*
.input entry

.decl prim(p:symbol)                         // P is in *primitives*
.input prim

.decl cap(c:symbol, p:symbol)                // capability C is gated by primitive P
.input cap

.decl portGlobal(v:symbol)                   // V is supplied by the port (*stinput*, *stoutput*)
.input portGlobal

.decl initprim(p:symbol)                     // primitives of the emitted initialiser (D7)
.input initprim

.decl userintern(f:symbol)                   // user defun F (or "top") mentions `intern`
.input userintern

.decl userglobal(f:symbol)                   // F has a (value X) / (set X _) with X not a literal symbol
.input userglobal

.decl readsIn(f:symbol, v:symbol)            // kernel defun F evaluates (value V), V a literal symbol
.input readsIn

.decl reads(n:number, v:symbol)              // the N-th kept toplevel form evaluates (value V)
.input reads

.decl writes(n:number, v:symbol)             // the N-th kept toplevel form evaluates (set V _)
.input writes

.decl portReads(v:symbol)                    // the port's runtime reads V natively (builders.json port_reads)
.input portReads

.decl defunwrite(v:symbol)                   // a kept DEFUN body evaluates (set V _)
.input defunwrite

// The runtime trace. Empty in an ordinary `yggdrasil facts` dump and filled
// by `yggdrasil trace-check` from a real run of the woven artifact. The
// filename is spelled out because stage 4 consumes readglobal.facts by that
// name (docs/analysis-rules.md, "Runtime trace").
.decl called(f:symbol)                       // the run entered F
.input called

.decl readGlobal(v:symbol)                   // the run evaluated (value V)
.input readGlobal(filename="readglobal.facts")

// ================================ mode =================================
// eval-free? tests the user KL's symbols against *eval-entry-points*. One
// hit is enough; evalcapable names every hit, which is what the
// "eval-capable because the program mentions:" line of `yggdrasil why`
// prints.

.decl evalcapable(s:symbol)
.output evalcapable
evalcapable(S) :- rawsym(S), entry(S).

// anyeval/evalfree are the boolean the rest of the rules branch on. They are
// one-column so that the negation below is over a ground atom.
.decl anyeval(x:number)
anyeval(1) :- evalcapable(_).

.decl evalfree(x:number)
evalfree(1) :- !anyeval(1).

// =============================== edges =================================
// One edge per kernel-defined symbol leaf of a body, in any position (D1),
// minus shen.f-error's whole row when the program is eval-free (D3).

.decl edge(f:symbol, g:symbol)
edge(F, G) :- callpos(F, G), kernel(G), F != "shen.f-error".
edge(F, G) :- argpos(F, G, _), kernel(G), F != "shen.f-error".
edge("shen.f-error", G) :- callpos("shen.f-error", G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).
edge("shen.f-error", G) :- argpos("shen.f-error", G, _), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

// =============================== seeds =================================
// The kernel's own toplevel init forms seed the floor; the user KL seeds the
// rest. Both readings of each are in the facts; the mode picks one (D4, D5).

.decl floorseed(g:symbol)
floorseed(G) :- formmentionsef(_, G), kernel(G), evalfree(1).
floorseed(G) :- formmentions(_, G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

.decl seed(g:symbol)
seed(G) :- floorseed(G).
seed(G) :- usersym(G), kernel(G), evalfree(1).
seed(G) :- rawsym(G), kernel(G), anyeval(1).

// ============================== reach ==================================
// The worklist traversal of yggdrasil.shen's `reach`, as least fixpoint.
// `reach` IS the acceptance criterion: it must equal kernel.kl's defun list
// minus shen.initialise. `floor` is the same traversal from the init forms
// alone -- what every program pays, the floor= figure of `yggdrasil why`.

.decl reach(g:symbol)
.output reach
reach(G) :- seed(G).
reach(G) :- reach(F), edge(F, G).

.decl floor(g:symbol)
.output floor
floor(G) :- floorseed(G).
floor(G) :- floor(F), edge(F, G).

// ====================== primitives and capabilities ====================
// find-primitives runs over the emitted code: the reachable defuns (after
// rewrite-f-error), the synthesised initialiser, and the user KL.

.decl usedprim(p:symbol)
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), reach(F), mentionsprim(F, P), F != "shen.f-error".
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), reach("shen.f-error"), mentionsprim("shen.f-error", P), anyeval(1).
// rewrite-f-error swaps the eval-free shen.f-error body for
// (simple-error (cn (str V) "...")), so those two primitives replace its own.
usedprim("simple-error") :- reach("shen.f-error"), evalfree(1).
usedprim("cn") :- reach("shen.f-error"), evalfree(1).
usedprim(P) :- initprim(P).
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), usersym(P), evalfree(1).
usedprim(P) :- prim(P), rawsym(P), anyeval(1).

// A capability is reachable exactly when the emitted KL mentions one of its
// gateway primitives -- the manifest's reaches= line.
.decl reaches(c:symbol)
.output reaches
reaches(C) :- cap(C, P), usedprim(P).

.decl needsEval(x:number)
.output needsEval
needsEval(1) :- usedprim("eval-kl").

// =========================== computed names ============================
// Stage 3's hypothesis, and the one rule here that decides nothing. The
// soundness argument for the whole shake is that a name the artifact can
// call occurs syntactically in the artifact. `intern` turns a string into a
// callable symbol; a (value X) / (set X _) whose X is not a literal symbol
// names a global only at runtime. Either one is outside the argument. It is
// NOT an eval entry point -- interning a name you never apply is harmless --
// so the shake only warns and records computed-names= in its manifests. The
// relation is here so the oracle and the Shen engine stay in lockstep about
// what the facts mean.

.decl computedName(f:symbol)
.output computedName
computedName(F) :- userintern(F).
computedName(F) :- userglobal(F).

// ============================ runtime trace ============================
// The empirical half of the reachability claim (docs/analysis-rules.md,
// "Runtime trace"). `reach` says what the artifact CAN call; `called` says
// what one run DID call. Soundness obligation 1 -- everything the artifact
// can call is in the footprint -- makes uncoveredCall necessarily empty, so
// a non-empty uncoveredCall is a counterexample to the shake itself, not to
// the trace.
//
// `kernel(F)` is what excludes user defuns, the weaver's own helpers and the
// synthesised shen.initialise: none of them is a call-graph row, so `reach`
// could not have derived them and a run entering them proves nothing.
//
// The converse containment does NOT hold and is not asserted: reach ⊋ called
// is ordinary imprecision plus the fact that one run takes one path.

.decl uncoveredCall(f:symbol)
.output uncoveredCall
uncoveredCall(F) :- called(F), kernel(F), !reach(F).

// initwrite is the globals some kept toplevel form writes -- exactly the
// projection of stage 4's `writes`, so it is derived rather than dumped as
// a fact file of its own.
.decl initwrite(v:symbol)
initwrite(V) :- writes(_, V).

// A global the run read that nothing in the artifact is known to write: no
// kept toplevel form (initwrite), no kept defun body (defunwrite), and no
// port global. defunwrite is what keeps the kernel's run-time counters --
// shen.*call*, shen.*infs*, shen.*gensym* -- out of the answer; it is
// deliberately NOT part of stage 4's liveGlobal, where only a toplevel write
// can be pruned.
.decl uncoveredRead(v:symbol)
.output uncoveredRead
uncoveredRead(V) :- readGlobal(V), !initwrite(V), !defunwrite(V), !portGlobal(V).

// portGlobal is unused by the stage-1 rules (uncoveredRead above is the
// first rule to read it): it is the input the stage-2 initialisation-order
// check (readBeforeWrite) needs, and dumping it keeps the fact set stable
// across that change.
.decl portGlobalUnused(v:symbol)
portGlobalUnused(V) :- portGlobal(V), V = "never".

// ======================= dead initialisation (stage 4) =================
// The synthesised initialiser writes a global for every kernel subsystem,
// most of which the shake has already removed. A global is LIVE when a
// REACHABLE kernel defun reads it, when a kept toplevel form reads it, when
// the port's own runtime reads it natively, or -- the over-approximation,
// in the shake's own symbol-based style -- when its name occurs anywhere in
// the user KL, in any position. Everything else is dead, and the form that
// writes it can go.
//
// D9. The note writes the user-KL clause as part of `liveGlobal(V) :-
//     usersym(V)`; here it is narrowed to globals some form actually writes,
//     which is the same answer for `deadInit` (the only consumer) over a far
//     smaller relation. `rawsym` rather than `usersym` because the question
//     is whether the NAME occurs in the program the user wrote, which is a
//     mode-independent fact -- strip-user-declares must not be able to make a
//     global look dead.
//
// D10. The rules decide only WHICH indices are dead. What is prunable is
//     narrower and is not a rule: the shake drops a form only when it is
//     exactly (set V Lit) for an atomic Lit. A form whose value is a call --
//     (set *property-vector* (vector 20000)) -- is an effect in its own
//     right, so it stays however dead its global is. See ygg.prunable? in
//     yggdrasil.shen; the oracle checks `deadInit`, not the pruning.

.decl liveGlobal(v:symbol)
.output liveGlobal
liveGlobal(V) :- readsIn(F, V), reach(F).
liveGlobal(V) :- reads(_, V).
liveGlobal(V) :- portReads(V).
liveGlobal(V) :- writes(_, V), rawsym(V).

.decl deadInit(n:number, v:symbol)
.output deadInit
deadInit(N, V) :- writes(N, V), !liveGlobal(V).
